85
00:06:49,530 --> 00:06:51,470
index type, and quantization.

86
00:06:52,600 --> 00:06:56,120
Broadly, there are two families of common vector index types these days.

87
00:06:56,520 --> 00:07:00,780
One is H&SW, which is memory-based, and the other is a disk-based index.

88
00:07:01,360 --> 00:07:07,520
The HNSW family builds a multi-layer graph, where each vector is connected to its nearest neighbors.

89
00:07:08,240 --> 00:07:14,400
This is the fast, high recall type of vector index with the requirement that the graph and the vectors need to fit in memory.

90
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:18,340
Then there are disk-based indexes like this BBQ.

91
00:07:18,760 --> 00:07:23,100
They take a fundamentally different approach to avoid this memory limitation of HNSW.

92
00:07:23,860 --> 00:07:31,740
This BBQ, for example, partitions vectors into clusters, and only puts the centroid into memory, leaving the actual vectors on disk.

93
00:07:31,900 --> 00:07:34,280
This hugely reduces the required memory.

94
00:07:34,700 --> 00:07:39,480
And while the search latency isn't as good with disk bbq with HNS in general, the difference

95
00:07:39,480 --> 00:07:40,460
isn't that big.

96
00:07:40,980 --> 00:07:46,940
And other operations like indexing or ingestion is actually faster with disk bbq than with HNSW.

97
00:07:47,910 --> 00:07:53,310
Both HNSW and disk bbq also have further detailed parameters for tuning the indexes.

98
00:07:53,790 --> 00:07:58,250
When it comes to HNSW, the key parameters are M, which defines how many connections

99
00:07:58,250 --> 00:08:02,490
each node can have in the graph, and EF construction, which is the size of

100
00:08:02,510 --> 00:08:05,230
candidates to keep in memory while building the graph.

101
00:08:06,330 --> 00:08:12,050
Moving to disk bbq, the key parameter is default visit percentage, which sets the default

102
00:08:12,050 --> 00:08:15,110
fraction of vectors to be visited per shard during search.

103
00:08:16,520 --> 00:08:22,200
And let's talk a little bit more about quantization. By default, vectors might be made of 32 bits each,

104
00:08:22,720 --> 00:08:27,120
but it turns out that a lot of that information can be just thrown away with fairly limited

105
00:08:27,120 --> 00:08:33,480
accuracy penalty. So we can actually only use 8 or 4 bits, or even a single bit with bbq or

106
00:08:33,600 --> 00:08:36,560
better binary quantization to represent each number.

107
00:08:37,460 --> 00:08:40,260
Quantization saves RAM and speeds up retrieval.

108
00:08:40,780 --> 00:08:45,560
Note that it doesn't necessarily save disk space as Elasticsearch always keeps the raw

109
00:08:45,560 --> 00:08:48,540
vectors on disk for rescoring. Speaking of which,

110
00:08:49,120 --> 00:08:53,480
rescoring in combination with oversampling is what vector search engines use these

111
00:08:53,480 --> 00:08:58,330
days to recover most of the lost information. At query time, let's say you ask for the

112
00:08:58,330 --> 00:09:03,670
top 10 results. Then, what a system like Elasticsearch does is to apply oversampling,

113
00:09:03,790 --> 00:09:06,650
grabbing additional like three times the required number.

114
00:09:07,010 --> 00:09:10,270
So that would be the top 30 candidates using the quantized vectors.

115
00:09:10,770 --> 00:09:16,590
It then rescores all 30 against the original unquantized vectors on disk and returns the best 10.

116
00:09:17,100 --> 00:09:21,000
For most data sets, this recovers nearly all of the recall loss.

117
00:09:21,970 --> 00:09:24,670
